1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,941
Chapter eight — the
physical and network lenses.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:14,034
Logical says what talks to what.
It does not say where anything is.

3
00:00:14,094 --> 00:00:21,539
Physical draws the domains:
edge, on-prem, Azure.

4
00:00:21,599 --> 00:00:32,044
Each node sits in the domain you assigned it,
and every link that crosses a frame is priced.

5
00:00:32,104 --> 00:00:45,543
Move a service to the cloud here and the
cost changes downstairs, immediately.

6
00:00:45,603 --> 00:00:54,050
Network is the wiring diagram a
network engineer would ask for.

7
00:00:54,110 --> 00:01:00,655
Protocol, port and bandwidth on every link.

8
00:01:00,715 --> 00:01:10,160
Per-domain egress, and exposure —
internal, private endpoint, or public.

9
00:01:10,220 --> 00:01:16,968
The same information is editable in one table.

10
00:01:17,028 --> 00:01:29,899
Network configuration: every connection, its
protocol, its port and the zones it crosses.

11
00:01:29,959 --> 00:01:36,646
And when a node needs sizing properly —

12
00:01:36,706 --> 00:01:48,151
The cluster sizer takes demand and an SLA and
returns nodes, partitions, replicas and licences.

13
00:01:48,211 --> 00:02:03,997
Elasticsearch, Cosmos, DynamoDB, Redis, Postgres,
Timescale, Kusto, Kafka — each with its own sizing model.

14
00:02:04,057 --> 00:02:09,461
One design. Ask it a different
question, get a different drawing.
